  "name": "weathervane torus",
  "description": "Chaos and overcrowding: every square is occupied from the start. Each side fields 24 pawns, a queen, two rooks, two knights, two bishops and a king, 32 pieces a side and 64 in all, scattered at random across the board, kings included; no pawn starts on the rank it would promote from. Standard chess rules with no castling. Nothing can move until a capture opens a square. The board is a torus: both file edges are joined and both rank edges are joined, for both players, so there is no edge and no corner. A pawn has a direction and may spend a whole move turning to face another way instead of moving; each starts pointing whichever way the position was rolled, so 'forward' is a fact about the piece rather than about the side. The starting position is random but contrived: rolled until neither king was in check, neither side had a mate in one, and the position played evenly and to a finish between the site's computer players.",
